From db2b689214c79a8affbd3b4d9ae4d01dbb81ffba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jingze Ma <jingzema0211@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 09:00:01 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] RT ck: down-sweep and up-sweep as parallel threads behind
 rt_ck_sweep_split (default off)

`rt_chain_ck` is the single most expensive kernel in the correlated-k hot-Jupiter runs:
4.531 ms/call and 30.1 % of GPU time on the cubed-sphere production profile
(bench/prof/cs_prof, 600 calls over 300 cycles), 5.511 ms and 40.0 % on spherical polar
(bench/prof/sp).  It is parallel over (m, chain block, k, j) only -- 12 MeshBlocks x 22
blocks x 16 x 16 = 67584 threads = 1056 wavefronts for 912 SIMDs -- and each thread runs
two radial recurrences in series over the 102 cells above the 10 bar cut (icut = 29,
ie = 129), carrying a private I_down[4][136] column, 4352 B/thread of scratch.

The two sweeps are INDEPENDENT.  The up-sweep starts from B_b(icut) (+ the internal flux),
which never involves I_down; the two meet only in the face flux
F_b(i) += w*(I_up(i) - I_down(i)), and that is a sum.  So each sweep can accumulate its own
half into its own slot and rt_apply's existing block sum puts them back together.

problem/rt_ck_sweep_split = true makes the sweep a parallel index: 2x the threads, half the
serial depth per thread, exactly the same arithmetic, and no private column at all (the
down-sweep only needs its running value, so this path has no NN tier).  F_b and Src are
written by both sweeps and get 2*nblk slots; Q_b is down-only and Em up-only and keep nblk,
so only two of rt_apply's four slot loops get longer.

Why this and not the alternatives: precomputing kappa or the layer coefficients per
(cell, chain) in a fully parallel kernel has been measured twice and lost both times
(-1 % and -18 %, the array traffic beats the saved work), caching the coefficients lost
120 %, and the k-table layout and blocked lookups were 0.5 % and 0.4 %.  All of those
attack the arithmetic.  This one attacks the only thing the measurements say is binding:
the kernel sustains 4.6x less work per second at 512 columns/GPU than at 8192, on the same
hardware and the same arithmetic, so parallelism is what it is short of.

NOT bitwise identical -- w*I_up and -w*I_down are summed separately instead of as
w*(I_up - I_down), and rt_apply now sums all down slots then all up slots.  Hence the flag,
default false.

Validation, serial CPU Release, restart of bench/cs_mhd_prod3 at cycle 710457:
  - tst/test_suite/rad/test_rad_dhj_ck_cpu.py passes (flag default off).
  - 20 cycles, flag on vs off: all 13 history columns (mass, 1/2/3-mom, tot-E, 1/2/3-KE,
    1/2/3-ME, dt) agree in every printed digit, max relative difference 0.0e+00.
  - 1 cycle, mhd_w_bcc dump: dens and bcc1 bitwise identical, everything else within one
    float32 ULP of the dump (max relative 2.1e-07 on bcc3, rms 1e-10), i.e. reassociation
    round-off and nothing else.

GPU A/B staged but not submitted: bench/prof/rt_new (submit.sh, NOTES.md), apudev, two
timed 300-cycle restarts plus the same two under rocprofv3, same method as
bench/prof/submit_new2.sh.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01V5CRX9dqkmuNz4bnP2W3ST
